Title: Reclaimed asphalt stimulates specialized microbiomes in urban Technosols
Authors: Elia Biagini, Serena Falcioni, Cecilia Polizzi, Leandro Di Gloria, Matteo Ramazzotti, Giulio Munz, Giacomo Certini

Abstract: 
Urban desealing aims at restoring permeable surfaces and enhancing ecosystem services but generates substantial quantities of waste asphalt.
The management of this material through landfilling represents a significant economic burden that often discourages restoration initiatives.
To address these challenges, the on-site integration of reclaimed asphalt into constructed soils has been proposed as a circular and cost-effective alternative.
This study evaluates the environmental viability of this practice, investigating whether asphalt triggers adverse effects on soil ecosystems through the release of hazardous substances.
Experimental soil mixtures with asphalt proportions of 10, 25 and 50% w/w, amended with 5% compost, were maintained in both liquid and solid phases.
Microbial functionality was assessed by measuring nitrification rates as a functional sentinel, alongside DNA metagenomics to identify taxonomic shifts.
Results showed no acute toxicity to nitrifying biomass; nitrification rates remained stable across all tests despite a relative abundance decline in detected ammonia-oxidizing archaea (AOA) at the highest asphalt dose.
While supernatant PAHs in ML and SL remained below the limit of quantification (< 0.1 mg/L), solid-phase exceedances for compounds such as benzo[a]pyrene occurred in asphalt-rich samples (25 and 50%).
DNA sequencing identified highly diverse communities, with a specialized enrichment of hydrocarbon degraders including Nocardia and Immundisolibacter.
These findings suggest that while incorporating high asphalt doses may challenge regulatory limits for soil, the functional resilience of the nitrogen cycle and the strong immobilization of contaminants support the feasibility of this approach.
However, low asphalt-to-soil ratios are advisable to ensure long-term microbial functionality and environmental safety in urban landscape design.
